<Table ID="Table1" XFormat="none" TableType="OneWay" EVFormat="AsteriskAfterNumber">
<Title>Col: Entering replicate data</Title>
<FloatingNote ID="Sticky0" Auto="0" Color="Yellow" Left="72" Top="300" Width="172" Height="849" ScrWidth="1920" ScrHeight="1080" ScrDPI="96">
<WebLink Flags="0" ToolTip="@help:column_tables" URL="@help:column_tables">
More about Column tables
</WebLink>

<B><Font Size="11" Color="#000000" Face="Arial">How the data are organized</Font></B>
<Font Size="11" Color="#000000" Face="Arial"><BR/>The three columns define three groups. The replicate values are stacked in each column</Font>
<B><Font Size="11" Color="#000000" Face="Arial"><BR/><BR/>How to graph these data</Font></B>
<Font Size="11" Color="#000000" Face="Arial"><BR/>Look in the navigator to the left and find the Graphs folder. Click on the graph in bold. Prism will present several choices for graphing these data. With small numbers of values (like here), it is best to create a scatter plot that shows every value. </Font>
<B><Font Size="11" Color="#000000" Face="Arial"><BR/><BR/>Notes</Font></B>
<Font Size="11" Color="#000000" Face="Arial"><BR/><BR/>-Unlike many statistics programs, Prism does not define groups using a grouping variable. Instead, the groups are defined by which column each value is entered into. </Font>
<Font Size="11" Color="#000000" Face="Arial"><BR/><BR/>-When you make a graph, you can choose to plot a column scatter graph (showing each value) or various kinds of graphs that summarize the data (bar graphs, box-and-whisker graphs, violin plots). </Font>
</FloatingNote>

<YColumn Width="81" Decimals="1" Subcolumns="1">
<Title>Accuracy</Title>
<Subcolumn>
<d>3.4</d>
<d>4.3</d>
<d>3</d>
<d>3.9</d>
<d>4.1</d>
</Subcolumn>
</YColumn>
<YColumn Width="81" Decimals="1" Subcolumns="1">
<Title>Confidence</Title>
<Subcolumn>
<d>2.3</d>
<d>5.2</d>
<d>4.5</d>
<d>3.1</d>
<d>5</d>
</Subcolumn>
</YColumn>
<YColumn Width="81" Decimals="1" Subcolumns="1">
<Title>Awareness</Title>
<Subcolumn>
<d>4.2</d>
<d>7.8</d>
<d>5.9</d>
<d>6.4</d>
<d>7.6</d>
</Subcolumn>
</YColumn>
<YColumn Width="82" Decimals="15" Subcolumns="1">
<Title>Unawareness</Title>
<Subcolumn/>
</YColumn>
</Table>
<Table ID="Table2" XFormat="none" YFormat="replicates" Replicates="3" TableType="TwoWay" EVFormat="AsteriskAfterNumber">
<Title>Grouped: Entering replicate data</Title>
<FloatingNote ID="Sticky1" Auto="0" Color="Yellow" Left="1736" Top="829" Width="173" Height="1518" ScrWidth="1920" ScrHeight="1080" ScrDPI="96">
<WebLink Flags="0" ToolTip="@help:bar_graphs" URL="@help:bar_graphs">
Learn about arranging data for different kinds of bar graphs
</WebLink>

<B><Font Size="11" Color="#000000" Face="Verdana">How grouped data are arranged</Font></B>
<Font Size="11" Color="#000000" Face="Verdana"><BR/>The columns define two treatments, and the rows define three genotypes. For each genotype, for each treatment, there are triplicate values entered in side by side subcolumns.  Note that one value is missing. Prism handles missing values without problem. </Font>
<Font Size="11" Color="#000000" Face="Verdana"><BR/><BR/>Unlike other programs, you do not define groups by entering values into special grouping variable columns.  Instead one grouping variable (treatment) is defined by columns, and the other grouping variable is defined by rows.</Font>
<B><Font Size="11" Color="#000000" Face="Verdana"><BR/><BR/>Analyzing the data</Font></B>
<Font Size="11" Color="#000000" Face="Verdana"><BR/>Click the Analyze button, and choose from the Grouped  analyses. Other sample data sets guide you through the three kinds of two-way ANOVA.</Font>
<B><Font Size="11" Color="#000000" Face="Verdana"><BR/><BR/>Grouped  bar graph</Font></B>
<Font Size="11" Color="#000000" Face="Verdana"><BR/>The linked graph is an interleaved bar graph.  Each column of data becomes one kind of bar (different appearance, defined with a legend). There are two kinds of bars (for columns A and B) and three of each type of bar (one for each genotype, defined by rows).   Error bars are computed from the scatter among side-by-side replicates entered in subcolumns. This is only one of several ways to create bar graphs with Prism.  Click the link below to learn about other kinds of bar graphs.</Font>
</FloatingNote>

<RowTitlesColumn Width="81">
<Subcolumn>
<d>Wild type</d>
<d>Knock-out A</d>
<d>Knock-out B</d>
</Subcolumn>
</RowTitlesColumn>
